@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Code 1: every language @@code runs)

`@@code(LANG, run)` gives an editor with a Run button. JavaScript and Python run in the browser; every other language runs on the server's sandboxed runner (or the student's own local runner).

@section(In the browser)

JavaScript runs in a Web Worker.

@code(javascript, run)
const xs = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5];
console.log(xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0));
@end

Python runs in Pyodide, with numpy available.

@code(python, run)
squares = [n * n for n in range(6)]
print(sum(squares))
@end

@section(On the server)

@box(w=100%, style=offline)
The blocks in this section need the IDoc server's runner or the student's local runner.
@end

C, compiled with gcc.

@code(c, run)
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    int s = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) s += i;
    printf("%d\n", s);
    return 0;
}
@end

C++, compiled with g++.

@code(cpp, run)
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>
int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3, 4};
    std::cout << std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0) << "\n";
}
@end

Rust.

@code(rust, run)
fn main() {
    let v: Vec<u32> = (1..=5).collect();
    println!("{}", v.iter().product::<u32>());
}
@end

Go.

@code(go, run)
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    fmt.Println(len("hello"))
}
@end

Java; the class with `main` is the program.

@code(java, run)
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(String.join("-", "a", "b", "c"));
    }
}
@end

Bash.

@code(bash, run)
for f in one two three; do echo "$f"; done | wc -l
@end

Lua.

@code(lua, run)
local t = {5, 7, 9}
print(#t, t[1] + t[3])
@end

Perl.

@code(perl, run)
my @w = split / /, "the quick brown fox";
print scalar(@w), "\n";
@end

PHP.

@code(php, run)
<?php
echo strtoupper("idoc"), "\n";
@end

R.

@code(r, run)
x <- c(2, 4, 6, 8)
cat(mean(x), sd(x), "\n")
@end

Ruby.

@code(ruby, run)
puts [3, 1, 2].sort.inspect
@end

Assembly (x86-64, GNU syntax), linked and run.

@code(assembly, run)
    .globl main
main:
    mov $42, %eax
    ret
@end

Verilog, simulated with Icarus Verilog.

@code(verilog, run)
module top;
  initial $display("%d", 4'b1010);
endmodule
@end

SystemVerilog.

@code(systemverilog, run)
module top;
  logic [7:0] a = 8'd200, b = 8'd55;
  initial $display("%0d", a + b);
endmodule
@end

@section(Listings that do not run)

Without `run` a block is a highlighted listing; `edit` makes it editable without running.

@code(python)
print("a listing only")
@end

@code(c, edit)
int main(void) { return 0; }
@end

Inline code sits in a sentence: @code(x = 1) is a short assignment.

@code(shell, edit)
$ make test
@end

@section(Older names that still work)

These spellings mean the same as a form of @@code; new documents should write @@code.

@codeblock(python)
print("codeblock")
@end

@listing(c)
int x = 1;
@end

@codebox(language=python)
print("codebox")
@end

@shellbox
$ ls
@end

@inlinecode(y = 2) is the older inline form.
